5️⃣ Minimum Number of Platforms (Greedy Scheduling)
📌 Why interviewers ask this

Asked in Amazon, Microsoft, Infosys

Tests greedy + sorting

Very similar to CPU scheduling / meeting rooms

Easy to explain, but logic must be precise

🧠 Problem Statement

Given two arrays:

arrival[] → arrival time of trains

departure[] → departure time of trains

Find the minimum number of platforms required so that no train waits.

✨ Example
Arrival   = [900, 940, 950, 1100, 1500, 1800]
Departure = [910, 1200, 1120, 1130, 1900, 2000]


Output

3

💡 Key Idea (Greedy)

👉 If one train arrives before another departs
➡️ we need one more platform

Otherwise:
➡️ one platform becomes free

🧩 Algorithm (Two Pointer + Sorting)

Sort arrival[]

Sort departure[]

Use two pointers i and j

If arrival[i] <= departure[j]:

Train arrives before one leaves → need platform

Else:

Train departs → free a platform

Track max platforms used

✅ Java Code
import java.util.Arrays;

public class MinimumPlatforms {

    public static int findPlatforms(int[] arr, int[] dep) {
        Arrays.sort(arr);
        Arrays.sort(dep);

        int platforms = 1;
        int maxPlatforms = 1;

        int i = 1, j = 0;

        while (i < arr.length && j < dep.length) {

            // Arrival before departure → need platform
            if (arr[i] <= dep[j]) {
                platforms++;
                i++;
            } 
            // Train departs → platform free
            else {
                platforms--;
                j++;
            }

            maxPlatforms = Math.max(maxPlatforms, platforms);
        }
        return maxPlatforms;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {900, 940, 950, 1100, 1500, 1800};
        int[] dep = {910, 1200, 1120, 1130, 1900, 2000};

        System.out.println(findPlatforms(arr, dep)); // Output: 3
    }
}

